### Why do traditional sessions break in microservices?

A classic monolith stores a session in server memory. In microservices with many instances behind a load balancer, a session in one instance is invisible to the others.

```java
package com.bank.session;

public class SessionProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Traditional session problem in microservices:");
        System.out.println("  Instance-1 creates session for Rajesh (sessionId=ABC)");
        System.out.println("  Next request hits Instance-2 via load balancer");
        System.out.println("  Instance-2 has NO session ABC -> 401 Unauthorized");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh appears logged out on every other request!");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Fix 1: Sticky sessions (defeats load balancing purpose)");
        System.out.println("  Fix 2: Shared session store in Redis (adds dependency)");
        System.out.println("  Fix 3: Stateless JWT (no server-side session at all) <- BEST");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Traditional session problem in microservices:
  Instance-1 creates session for Rajesh (sessionId=ABC)
  Next request hits Instance-2 via load balancer
  Instance-2 has NO session ABC -> 401 Unauthorized
  Rajesh appears logged out on every other request!
```

### How does stateless JWT authentication work?

On login, the identity provider issues a signed JWT. The client stores it and sends it on every request. Each service validates locally.

```java
package com.bank.session;

public class StatelessJwtFlow {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Stateless JWT flow:");
        System.out.println("  1. POST /auth/login {user: rajesh, password: ****}");
        System.out.println("  2. Auth service verifies credentials");
        System.out.println("  3. Auth service mints JWT: {sub: rajesh, roles: [CUSTOMER], exp: 5min}");
        System.out.println("  4. Client stores JWT");
        System.out.println("  5. GET /accounts/SAV2001  Authorization: Bearer <jwt>");
        System.out.println("  6. Account service validates JWT locally (signature + expiry)");
        System.out.println("  7. POST /transfers       Authorization: Bearer <jwt>");
        System.out.println("  8. Transfer service validates same JWT locally");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("No server-side session. No shared state. Any instance handles any request.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Stateless JWT flow:
  1. POST /auth/login {user: rajesh, password: ****}
  2. Auth service verifies credentials
  3. Auth service mints JWT: {sub: rajesh, roles: [CUSTOMER], exp: 5min}
  4. Client stores JWT
  5. GET /accounts/SAV2001  Authorization: Bearer <jwt>
  6. Account service validates JWT locally (signature + expiry)
  7. POST /transfers       Authorization: Bearer <jwt>
  8. Transfer service validates same JWT locally

No server-side session. No shared state. Any instance handles any request.
```

### How does each service validate the token?

The service checks the signature using the public key, confirms expiry, and verifies issuer and audience.

```java
package com.bank.session;

import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.http.SessionCreationPolicy;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
public class ServiceSecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .oauth2ResourceServer(oauth2 -> oauth2
                .jwt(jwt -> jwt
                    .jwkSetUri("https://auth.nbi.com/.well-known/jwks.json")
                ))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .anyRequest().authenticated());
        return http.build();
    }
}
```

```text
Validation checks per request:
  1. Fetch public key from JWKS endpoint (cached)
  2. Verify signature: HMAC or RSA
  3. Check exp: not expired
  4. Check iss: matches expected issuer
  5. Check aud: matches this service
  6. Extract sub and roles -> set SecurityContext
```

### Why does this scale?

Because everything needed is in the token. Any instance of any service validates locally. Add or remove instances freely.

```java
package com.bank.session;

public class WhyItScales {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Scalability with JWT:");
        System.out.println("  Account service: 5 instances");
        System.out.println("  Transfer service: 3 instances");
        System.out.println("  Statement service: 10 instances");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Rajesh's request can hit ANY instance of ANY service");
        System.out.println("  Because the JWT carries his identity");
        System.out.println("  No shared session store needed");
        System.out.println("  Scale up: just add instances. Scale down: just remove.");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Scalability with JWT:
  Account service: 5 instances
  Transfer service: 3 instances
  Statement service: 10 instances

  Rajesh's request can hit ANY instance of ANY service
  Because the JWT carries his identity
  No shared session store needed
```

### What is the revocation tradeoff?

A JWT is self-contained and validated locally. There is no central place to invalidate it, so it stays valid until expiry.

```java
package com.bank.session;

public class RevocationTradeoff {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Revocation problem:");
        System.out.println("  Rajesh logs out, but his JWT has 5 minutes left");
        System.out.println("  An attacker with the stolen token can use it for 5 minutes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Mitigations:");
        System.out.println("  1. Short-lived access tokens: 5 minutes");
        System.out.println("  2. Refresh tokens: revoke on logout -> no new access tokens");
        System.out.println("  3. Refresh rotation: detect reuse -> revoke entire family");
        System.out.println("  4. Denylist: for immediate revocation, check jti against small list");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Revocation problem:
  Rajesh logs out, but his JWT has 5 minutes left
  An attacker with the stolen token can use it for 5 minutes

Mitigations:
  1. Short-lived access tokens: 5 minutes
  2. Refresh tokens: revoke on logout -> no new access tokens
  3. Refresh rotation: detect reuse -> revoke entire family
  4. Denylist: for immediate revocation, check jti against small list
```

### How does RS256 help in multi-service systems?

The auth service signs with a private key. Every service verifies with the public key from a JWKS endpoint. Services cannot mint tokens.

```java
package com.bank.session;

public class Rs256Benefit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("RS256 in multi-service:");
        System.out.println("  Auth service: holds PRIVATE key, signs tokens");
        System.out.println("  Account service: fetches PUBLIC key from JWKS, verifies");
        System.out.println("  Transfer service: fetches PUBLIC key from JWKS, verifies");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("  Benefit: services CANNOT mint tokens (no private key)");
        System.out.println("  A compromised service can verify but not forge tokens");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
RS256 in multi-service:
  Auth service: holds PRIVATE key, signs tokens
  Account service: fetches PUBLIC key from JWKS, verifies
  Transfer service: fetches PUBLIC key from JWKS, verifies

  Benefit: services CANNOT mint tokens (no private key)
  A compromised service can verify but not forge tokens
```

### When would you use a shared session store instead?

Rarely, but if you need immediate revocation on every request, a shared store like Redis gives you that at the cost of a dependency.

```java
package com.bank.session;

public class WhenToUseRedisSession {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Use JWT (stateless) when:");
        System.out.println("  - Standard microservices architecture");
        System.out.println("  - Horizontal scaling is important");
        System.out.println("  - 5-minute token TTL is acceptable for revocation");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Use Redis session (stateful) when:");
        System.out.println("  - Immediate revocation on EVERY request is required");
        System.out.println("  - Regulatory requirement for instant session kill");
        System.out.println("  - Accept the shared dependency and latency cost");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Use JWT (stateless) when:
  - Standard microservices architecture
  - Horizontal scaling is important
  - 5-minute token TTL is acceptable for revocation

Use Redis session (stateful) when:
  - Immediate revocation on EVERY request is required
  - Regulatory requirement for instant session kill
  - Accept the shared dependency and latency cost
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Never store sensitive data in the token. Always send over TLS. Validate audience to prevent cross-service replay. Propagate a correlation ID for tracing.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] JWT for stateless, scalable authentication
  [x] RS256 so services verify without holding signing secret
  [x] Short-lived access tokens (5 min) + refresh rotation
  [x] Validate iss, aud, exp on every request
  [x] Never store sensitive data in payload (only encoded, not encrypted)
  [x] Always over TLS (bearer credential)
  [x] Propagate correlation/trace ID alongside token
  [x] Optional denylist for forced immediate revocation
```
